From d1d6a47c223875ed031e723365e8d58a3a281705 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Garm Lucassen <garm.lucassen@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 20:49:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(output-savings): measure only provenance-qualified
 observations

Review follow-up: the cluster gate was a per-stratum admission test, so the
five conversations that cleared it also admitted every legacy request already
sitting in that stratum's mean and variance. Loading an old stratum of 2,500
unattributed requests per arm and then recording five matched conversations
per arm flipped the estimate from None to "measured -99.80% over 2,505
treatment requests", while the labelled conversations underneath showed no
difference at all.

Split each arm accumulator into two views instead of gating one. The totals
(n/sum/sumsq) keep every observation and keep feeding the estimated tier,
which is reported as an estimate and never claimed to be causal. A separate
`qualified` sub-accumulator holds only observations that arrived with a
conversation label, carries the cluster set, and is the only view
estimate_from_holdout reads.

That makes provenance structural rather than a check: unattributed traffic has
nowhere to land in the measured path, so it cannot ride in behind a stratum
that later qualifies, and it cannot be added to one that already has. Clusters
are now counted over exactly the observations whose mean and variance they
vouch for. Ledgers written before provenance existed deserialize with
qualified=None and stay out of the measured estimate permanently, since their
sums cannot be split after the fact.

Covers the upgrade path end to end: legacy load reaching the five-conversation
threshold, unlabelled traffic arriving after a stratum qualifies, save/load of
a mixed ledger, the same path through the live label channel, and merge.

